-- title:
--   Normal fibre model of X₀(N) with cusp chart
-- statement:
--   Fix $N\ge 1$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, a prime $\ell$, a field $k$ of characteristic $\ell$, and a surjective ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red}\colon A\to k$. Assume given modular polynomial data at level $N$, i.e. a monic $\Phi\in\mathbb{Z}[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(N)=\sum_{d\mid N,\ d\text{ squarefree}}N/d$ in $Y$ annihilating the $q$-expansion pair $(j,j_N)$, whose coefficients satisfy $\deg(\Phi_i)\le N(\psi(N)-i)$ for all $i$; assume the relative degree of $k(\tilde\jmath,\tilde\jmath_N)=k(\mathtt{jqModC}\,k,\mathtt{jqNModC}\,k\,N)\subset k((q))$ over $k(\tilde\jmath)$ equals $\psi(N)$; and assume two lifting hypotheses: every $y\in k(\tilde\jmath,\tilde\jmath_N)$ admitting a monic $P\in k[X][Y]$ with $P(\tilde\jmath,y)=0$ (respectively $P(\tilde\jmath^{-1},y)=0$) is the image, under the reduction homomorphism $\mathtt{modularRedLocHom}$ from the localisation of the subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ generated by the constants $A$, $j$ and $j_N$ at the complement of the reduction kernel, of an element integral in the same monic two-variable sense over $A[j]$ (respectively over $A[j^{-1}]$). Then there exists a fibre model $fm$ of level $N$ over $A$ reducing along $\mathrm{red}$ — chart subrings $B_{\mathrm{fin}}\ni \bar\jmath,\bar\jmath_N$ and $B_{\mathrm{inf}}\ni\bar\jmath^{-1}$ of the base change of the full modular function field, integral over the respective affine bases, with reduction maps $\pi_{\mathrm{fin}},\pi_{\mathrm{inf}}$ into $k(\tilde\jmath,\tilde\jmath_N)$ satisfying the compatibilities recorded in `FibreModel` — which carries a cusp chart, that is, $\bar\jmath_N(\bar\jmath^{-1})^N\in B_{\mathrm{inf}}$ with $\pi_{\mathrm{inf}}$-image $\tilde\jmath_N\tilde\jmath^{-N}$, and for which both $B_{\mathrm{fin}}$ and $B_{\mathrm{inf}}$ are integrally closed.
--
--   This is the normality statement for a model of $X_0(N)$ over a valuation ring: the two affine charts of the model may be chosen integrally closed while retaining the cusp chart at infinity, which classically corresponds to the normal Kroneckerian model of the field of elliptic modular functions. Integral closedness of the chart subrings is the input needed for divisor specialisation on the model to behave well, and the statement is used in the treatment of Hecke divisors on fibre models and in the prime-level version of the existence result.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_CharPModel_exists_fibreModel_cuspChart_integrallyClosed.lean

